.TH maptool 1  "$Date$" "$Revision$" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
maptool \- parse osm textfile and convert to Navit binfile format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B For OSM XML data:
.B bzcat planet.osm.bz2 | maptool mymap.bin
[\-h] [\-6] [\-a <level>] [\-c] [\-C <method>] [\-d <connect string]
[\-e <phase>] [\-i <file>] [\-k] [\-M] [\-N] [\-o] [\-r <file>] [\-s <phase>]
[\-S <size>] [\-T <threads>] [\-w] [\-W] [\-U] [\-z <level>]

.B For OSM Protobuf/PBF data:
.B maptool \-\-protobuf \-i planet.osm.pbf planet.bin
[\-h] [\-6] [\-a <level>] [\-c] [\-C <method>] [\-e <phase>]
[\-i <file>] [\-k] [\-M] [\-N] [\-o] [\-P] [\-r <file>] [\-s <phase>]
[\-S <size>] [\-T <threads>] [\-w] [\-W] [\-U] [\-z <level>]
.SH DESCRIPTION
maptool parses osm textfile and converts it to Navit binfile format
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-h (\-\-help)
display a short help message
.TP
\-6 (\-\-64bit)
set zip 64 bit compression
.TP
\-a (\-\-attr-debug-level) <level>
control which data is included in the debug attribute
.TP
\-c (\-\-dump-coordinates)
dump coordinates after phase 1
.TP
\-C (\-\-compression-method) <method>
select compression method: zlib or lzma (default zlib).
lzma is only available if maptool was built with liblzma support.
.TP
\-d (\-\-db) <connect string>
get osm data out of a postgresql database with osm simple scheme and given connect string
.TP
\-D (\-\-dump)
dump map data to standard output in Navit textfile format
If this option is specified, the output file parameter must be omitted.
.TP
\-e (\-\-end) <phase>
end at specified phase
.TP
\-E (\-\-experimental)
enable experimental features (if available)
.TP
\-i (\-\-input-file) <file>
specify the input file name (OSM), overrules default stdin
.TP
\-k (\-\-keep-tmpfiles)
do not delete tmp files after processing. useful to reuse them
.TP
\-M (\-\-o5m)
input data is in o5m format
.TP
\-n (\-\-ignore-unknown)
do not output ways and nodes with unknown type
.TP
\-N (\-\-nodes-only)
process only nodes
.TP
\-\-ocean-polygons <file>
Replace coastline inference with prepared ocean polygons, including island
holes. The file is a native-endian Navit item stream containing only closed
poly_water_tiled polygons and poly_hole attributes. Invalid or truncated items
are fatal errors; an empty file represents a region with no ocean.
.PP
Prepare this file with contrib/coastline-polygons.py, using the full-resolution
global land-polygons-split-3857 dataset from https://osmdata.openstreetmap.de/
and the geographic bounds of the map. Use the same pinned global snapshot for
maps loaded together. The preparation tool validates topology, converts the
projection and preserves holes when rounding to Navit's integer coordinates.
Its Python dependencies are needed only on the conversion host.
Prepared ocean items are indexed at depth four or shallower so their coverage
is available at every display order, including overview zooms.
.PP
This option replaces all ocean generated from the regional OSM coastlines.
Roads, inland water, search indexes and other OSM features follow the normal
conversion path. Without this option, the historical coastline inference is
still used and can flood land when regional coastlines are incomplete.
Use the option consistently when resuming maptool phases.
.TP
\-P (\-\-protobuf)
input data is in pbf (Protocol Buffer) format
.TP
\-r (\-\-rule-file) <file>
read mapping rules from specified file
.TP
\-s (\-\-start) <phase>
start at specified phase
.TP
\-S (\-\-slice-size) <phrase>
limit memory to use for some large internal buffers, in bytes. Default is 1 GB.
Smaller slices reduce peak memory usage, at the cost of increased processing time.
.TP
\-t (\-\-timestamp) <y-m-dTh:m:s>
set zip timestamp
.TP
\-T (\-\-threads) <count>
number of worker threads for tile compression (default: auto-detect CPU count).
Set to 1 to disable threading.
.TP
\-w (\-\-dedupe-ways)
ensure no duplicate ways or nodes. useful when using several input files
.TP
\-W (\-\-ways-only)
process only ways
.TP
\-U (\-\-unknown-country)
add objects with unknown country to index
.TP
\-x (\-\-index-size)
set maximum country index size in bytes
.TP
\-z (\-\-compression-level) <level>
set the compression level (1-9 for zlib, 1-9 for lzma, default 6)
.SH WATER AREAS
Standalone water areas must contain at least three vertices and return to the
same OSM node. Open water, reservoir and basin ways are retained for
multipolygon assembly, but are not filled independently. This avoids
inventing water across land or islands by closing an incomplete boundary.
Multipolygon rings are assembled from connected members and classified by
containment, including nested islands and water bodies. Member roles do not
prevent connected segments from forming a ring. Inner-only roots remain land
when their enclosing shore is absent. Water relations with missing or unclosed
inner members are skipped entirely, with their OSM relation URL in a warning.
Open rings are not closed or repaired: check source data and extract completeness
before correcting the source. There is no list of excluded OSM object IDs.
Valid geometry beyond the legacy tile grid remains indexed without changing
tile names. Existing binfiles must be rebuilt to apply these fixes.
